{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["And the word <b>חמד</b> in the sacred language is interpreted in two ways. The first: robbery, oppression, and taking what belongs to others by force and coercion — and so 'and he shall not covet (לא יחמוד) your land' (Exodus 34:24); if its meaning were not so, the land would be bad, and the verse comes only to praise. The second way: desire in the heart, not going forth into action. Now, 'you shall not covet (לא תחמוד) your neighbor's house' — the ninth commandment. 'You shall not covet (לא תחמוד) your neighbor's wife' — the tenth commandment. And 'your neighbor's house' and 'your neighbor's wife' may be joined to each other, or 'your neighbor's house' is a general category. The proof is that Moses, in this book which expounded the Torah, said in place of לא תחמוד: לא תתאוה (you shall not desire).\n\nAnd many have said that there is no sin in the thought of the heart, and there is neither reward nor punishment for it, and there are many proofs to refute them, but I shall not lengthen. I shall only show them 'a heart devising wicked thoughts' — the good things — for 'it was with your heart' and 'and the upright in their hearts,' and Moses said at the end, 'in your mouth and in your heart, to do it.' And the essence of all the commandments is to straighten the heart. Most of them apply to one who acts intentionally, and the inadvertent and the intentional prove it."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 5, :notes {0 "The phrase הטיבות is textually difficult; translated hyper-literally as 'the good things,' though the syntax is disrupted and the reading may be corrupt."}, :source-witnesses [["On Your Way" "http://mobile.tora.ws/"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:22:30.149533294Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}